# A language server in a child process

Most languages already have a language server: a program that answers an editor's questions about the language over the Language Server Protocol, LSP for short. Rust has rust-analyzer, Python has Pyright, Go has gopls, and TypeScript 7 has `tsc --lsp`. Loom does not rebuild those servers. A language's package starts its server as a child process and asks it about the composed code, the same way an editor would ask about a file on disk.

`LanguageServerProcess` is that bridge, and nothing in it knows which language it serves. It starts a server, keeps the files it is given open in the server, and sends the server requests. TypeScript 7 is the first package to use it, and the package for any other language reuses it unchanged.

## What the bridge offers

A language server answers only about files it knows, and a composed file exists nowhere on disk, or exists there as an older tangle. So the bridge sends each file's text to the server as an open document, which the server reads in place of the disk. `keepOpen` takes the whole set of files that should be open, each an `OpenFile` with its path and text. It opens the files new to the set, sends the new text of the files that changed, and closes the files no longer in it. Every call names the full set, so the caller never tracks what the server has already seen.

`request` sends one request and resolves with the server's answer. `alive` says whether the server is still running, so a caller can turn to another source of answers when it is not. `dispose` asks the server to shut down and stops the process.

=>

export interface OpenFile {
  readonly path: string
  readonly text: string
}

export interface LanguageServerProcess {
  readonly keepOpen: (files: ReadonlyArray<OpenFile>) => void
  readonly request: <A>(method: string, params: unknown) => Promise<A>
  readonly alive: () => boolean
  readonly dispose: () => void
}

## Starting the server

`startLanguageServer` starts the server from a command and its arguments, in the folder the server should treat as its workspace, and speaks to it over the process's standard input and output with `vscode-jsonrpc`. It tells the server the language of the files it will open, and what the client can do, as LSP's `initialize` request asks.

The bridge imports `vscode-jsonrpc` only when it starts a server. A workspace that never runs a language server never loads the package, and the service itself loads without it, the way it loads without Prettier until it formats. `connected` is the promise of the connection, ready once the import arrives.

A server may receive nothing before it has answered `initialize`. So the bridge keeps `ready`, the promise of that answer, and sends every later message only once `ready` settles. Each message is attached to `ready` in the order the caller sent it, and a promise runs its callbacks in the order they were attached, so the server receives them in that order too. An open document always reaches the server before a request about it.

A server that fails to start, or exits later, is no longer alive. `alive` reports it, and every request still waiting rejects, so the caller hears of the failure at once rather than waiting for an answer that will never come.

=>

export const startLanguageServer = (
  command: string,
  args: ReadonlyArray<string>,
  workspace: string,
  languageId: string,
  capabilities: object,
): LanguageServerProcess => {
  const child = spawn(command, [...args], { cwd: workspace, stdio: ['pipe', 'pipe', 'ignore'] })
  const connected: Promise<MessageConnection> = import('vscode-jsonrpc/node').then((rpc) => {
    const connection = rpc.createMessageConnection(
      new rpc.StreamMessageReader(child.stdout),
      new rpc.StreamMessageWriter(child.stdin),
    )
    connection.onRequest(() => null)
    connection.listen()
    return connection
  })
  const state = { running: true }
  const stop = (): void => {
    state.running = false
    void connected.then((connection) => connection.dispose()).catch(() => undefined)
  }
  child.on('exit', stop)
  child.on('error', stop)

  const ready: Promise<MessageConnection> = connected.then((connection) =>
    connection
      .sendRequest('initialize', {
        processId: process.pid,
        rootUri: URI.file(workspace).toString(),
        workspaceFolders: [{ uri: URI.file(workspace).toString(), name: basename(workspace) }],
        capabilities,
      })
      .then(() => connection.sendNotification('initialized', {}))
      .then(() => connection),
  )

~

`keepOpen` compares the set it is given with `opened`, the text and version of every file the server holds open. A file not yet in `opened` is sent with `didOpen` at version one. A file whose text differs is sent whole with `didChange`, under the next version. A file in `opened` but not in the new set is closed with `didClose`. `opened` changes at once, while the messages wait for `ready`, so a second call before the server answers compares with what the first call sent.

=>

  const opened = new Map<string, { readonly text: string; readonly version: number }>()
  const uriOf = (path: string): string => URI.file(path).toString()
  const send = (method: string, params: unknown): void =>
    void ready.then((connection) => connection.sendNotification(method, params)).catch(() => undefined)

  const keepOpen = (files: ReadonlyArray<OpenFile>): void => {
    const wanted = new Map(Array.map(files, (file) => [file.path, file.text] as const))
    Array.fromIterable(opened.keys())
      .filter((path) => !wanted.has(path))
      .forEach((path) => {
        opened.delete(path)
        send('textDocument/didClose', { textDocument: { uri: uriOf(path) } })
      })
    files.forEach((file) => {
      const held = opened.get(file.path)
      if (held === undefined) {
        opened.set(file.path, { text: file.text, version: 1 })
        send('textDocument/didOpen', {
          textDocument: { uri: uriOf(file.path), languageId, version: 1, text: file.text },
        })
      } else if (held.text !== file.text) {
        const version = held.version + 1
        opened.set(file.path, { text: file.text, version })
        send('textDocument/didChange', {
          textDocument: { uri: uriOf(file.path), version },
          contentChanges: [{ text: file.text }],
        })
      }
    })
  }

~

`request` waits for `ready` like every other message, then sends the request. A server that is no longer running rejects the request at once.

=>

  return {
    keepOpen,
    request: <A>(method: string, params: unknown): Promise<A> =>
      state.running
        ? ready.then((connection) => connection.sendRequest<A>(method, params))
        : Promise.reject(new Error(`the ${command} language server is not running`)),
    alive: () => state.running,
    dispose: () => {
      if (!state.running) return
      void ready
        .then((connection) =>
          connection.sendRequest('shutdown').then(() => connection.sendNotification('exit')),
        )
        .catch(() => undefined)
        .finally(() => {
          stop()
          child.kill()
        })
    },
  }
}

## What the bridge draws on

Node's `child_process` starts the server, and `path` names its workspace folder. `vscode-jsonrpc` frames the messages on the process's standard streams and pairs each answer with its request. It is a dependency the service imports at run time rather than bundles, for the reason the chapter on bundling the service gives, and its type alone is imported at the top, since the module itself arrives only when a server starts. `URI` turns a path into the `file:` address the protocol names a document by. Effect's `Array` builds the set of files the caller wants open.

=>

import { Array } from 'effect'
import { spawn } from 'node:child_process'
import { basename } from 'node:path'
import type { MessageConnection } from 'vscode-jsonrpc/node'
import { URI } from 'vscode-uri'

